# zenresize with_mask cost on the I16Srgb config — the f32 fallback is NOT a bottleneck on aarch64
date:  2026-08-27
host:  Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build: bench profile (release), NO -C target-cpu=native, runtime dispatch (incant!)
lib:   zenresize @ ed8e5c33 + benches/mask_e2e.rs (this commit)
bench: nice -n 19 cargo bench -j 4 --bench mask_e2e
issue: https://github.com/imazen/zenresize/issues/3

## Question

Issue #3 proposes applying masks on the i16 paths so that `with_mask()` no longer
forces the f32 path ("avoids f32 conversion overhead for the common case of
rounded corners + solid background -> JPEG"). Before building that, measure what
the f32 fallback actually costs. Both arms use the same RGBA8_SRGB `.srgb()`
config (opaque gradient input, Lanczos); `no_mask` takes I16Srgb, `rounded_mask`
takes F32 (paths printed by the bench, verified in the log).

## Result (in-run paired ratio, rounded_mask vs no_mask)

| scenario         | no_mask (I16Srgb) | rounded_mask (F32) | 95% CI vs no_mask |
|------------------|-------------------|--------------------|-------------------|
| 4K -> 1080p      | 79.7 ±1.4 ms      | 71.3 ±1.5 ms       | -11.4% .. -9.6%   |
| 1080p -> 4K up   | 79.3 ±1.9 ms      | 57.3 ±1.9 ms       | -29.1% .. -26.5%  |
| 800x600 -> 400x300 | 8.0 ±1.1 ms     | 8.6 ±0.9 ms        | +5.7% .. +10.2%   |

The f32 path WITH a mask is faster than the i16 path WITHOUT one at >= 1080p on
this machine; it costs ~0.6 ms (+8%) on the small image. So on aarch64 an i16
mask path would make masked resizes slower at production sizes, and less precise
(u8 premultiplied double rounding vs the f32 path's single rounding).

The I16Srgb kernels are NEON-vectorised here (`filter_h_u8_to_i16_neon`,
`filter_v_row_i16_neon`) — this is not a missing-kernel artefact.

## Sanity check

A first run accidentally left the builder default `linear = true`, which puts
BOTH arms on F32 (RGBA straight alpha needs premul -> not I16Linear). That run
measured 1.00x on all three scenarios (CI crossing zero), i.e. the mask itself
is free on the f32 path; the bench now prints each arm's `working_format()` so a
flat ratio cannot be mistaken for "the mask is free" again.

## Not measured

x86-64 (AVX2 / AVX-512). CLAUDE.md records I16Srgb as "~2x faster" than f32,
which was measured on x86. If the i16 path is faster there, the i16 mask path
may still pay on x86 — run this bench on the 7950X before deciding. Absolute
timings drift between runs on this machine; only the in-run ratio is comparable.
